{"name":"Rope","hex":"#8e593c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rope","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Thick strings, yarn, monofilaments, metal wires, or strands of other cordage that are twisted together to form a stronger line.","An individual length of such material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To tie (something) with rope.","To throw a rope (or something similar, e.g. a lasso, cable, wire, etc.) around (something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rope"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rope","blocks":[{"text":"A rope is a group of yarns, plies, fibres, or strands that are twisted or braided together into a larger and stronger form. Ropes have high tensile strength and can be used for dragging and lifting. Rope is thicker and stronger than similarly constructed cord, string, and twine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Cordage_en_chanvre.jpg/330px-Cordage_en_chanvre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rope"},{"title":"Rope (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Rope is a 1948 American psychological crime thriller film directed by Alfred Hitchcock, inspired by the 1929 play of the same title by Patrick Hamilton. The film was adapted by Hume Cronyn with a screenplay by Arthur Laurents."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8e/Rope2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rope_(film)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/SuperMacro_Rope.JPG/250px-SuperMacro_Rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/SuperMacro_Rope.JPG/500px-SuperMacro_Rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/SuperMac